IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- aia-core: the shared nouns of the EU Artificial Intelligence Act,
-- Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 as amended by Regulation (EU) 2026/1744 (the Omnibus).
-- Types, constructors and two constants only. No rules (the rules are in the other aia- modules).
-- Every part reads this file and never edits it. A part that lacks a type defines it under its
-- own provision label and records it in NEEDS-CORE.md.
-- Test for membership: could a witness of ordinary competence testify to this fact, or does
-- answering it need the Regulation? Only the first kind is here. Money is a NUMBER of euros.
-- Dates are DATE, written `Date day month year` (day first); there is no YMD of ours.

§ `The two versions of the Regulation`

-- The Regulation as enacted (OJ L, 12.7.2024) governs the dates before 27 July 2026; the
-- Regulation as amended by the Omnibus governs from 27 July 2026 (the Omnibus's Article 4;
-- aia-timeline.l4 computes the date).
DECLARE `Version of the Regulation` IS ONE OF
    `as enacted`
    `as amended`

§ `The provision groups whose date of application differs (Article 113 as amended)`

-- One constructor for each set of provisions that Article 113, or Article 111(2)'s saving of
-- Article 5, treats alike. PLAN.md section 3 says which Articles and Annexes belong to which.
DECLARE `Provision group` IS ONE OF
    `Chapter I`                                                  -- Articles 1 to 4a (Art 113 third paragraph, point (a))
    `Chapter II`                                                 -- Article 5, except the additions below (point (a))
    `Article 5 additions`                                        -- Art 5(1) first subparagraph points (ba), (bb); Art 5(1a), (1b) (point (a), exception)
    `Notified bodies, GPAI, governance, penalties and Art 78`    -- Chapter III Section 4, Chapters V, VII, XII except Art 101, and Art 78 (point (b))
    `Chapter III Sections 1 to 3`                                -- classification, requirements, obligations, except Art 6(5) (point (c); the date depends on the route)
    `Articles 102 to 110`                                        -- the amendments of other acts (point (d))
    `General`                                                    -- every other provision: Art 113 second paragraph (includes Art 6(5), Art 101, Chapter III Section 5)

§ `The route by which an AI system is high-risk`

-- Article 6(1) with Annex I, or Article 6(2) with Annex III, or neither. The answer comes
-- from the classification in Article 6 (part P1); a seat that does not know it asks the user,
-- and a layer-1 function takes it as an input named for Article 6.
DECLARE `Route to high-risk` IS ONE OF
    `Route Article 6(1) and Annex I`
    `Route Article 6(2) and Annex III`
    `Route neither`

§ `Roles`

-- The roles the text names. `operator` stands for any of the six roles of Article 3(8); a
-- person states the role they hold, never `operator`. `notified body` and `affected person`
-- are roles of the Regulation but not operators (Article 3(8) closes its list with "means").
DECLARE Role IS ONE OF
    Provider
    Deployer
    `Authorised representative`
    Importer
    Distributor
    `Product manufacturer`
    Operator
    `Notified body`
    `Affected person`

§ `Where a party is`

-- The place facts the text turns on (Article 2(1), Article 3(5), (6)). Only "in the Union" and
-- "in a third country" appear in the text; a party in neither is outside the Regulation's
-- place tests.
DECLARE `Place of a party` IS ONE OF
    `Established or located in the Union`
    `Established or located in a third country`

§ `Facts about a party, an AI system and an AI model`

-- A party is any natural or legal person, public authority, agency or other body.
DECLARE Party HAS
    `natural person`                        IS A BOOLEAN
    `public authority, agency or other body` IS A BOOLEAN
    `place`                                 IS A `Place of a party`

-- An AI system as the time axis sees it. Part agents who take a system as one input use this
-- record and pass its fields to aia-timeline.l4's applicability function.
DECLARE `AI system` HAS
    `date placed on the market or put into service` IS A DATE   -- the earlier of the two dates
    `route`                                        IS A `Route to high-risk`
    `significantly changed in its design since the date of application of Chapter III` IS A BOOLEAN -- Art 111(2): "only if, as from that date, those systems are subject to significant changes in their designs"

-- An AI model as the time axis and Article 51 see it. Compute is in floating-point operations, as a NUMBER.
DECLARE `AI model` HAS
    `date placed on the market`                                 IS A DATE
    `cumulative compute used for its training, in floating-point operations` IS A NUMBER

-- A reference to a provision, for a function that asks "does Article N apply?". Paragraph 0
-- means the whole Article; letter is "" or "a", "b", "c", "d" (Articles 4a, 60a, 75a to 75d).
DECLARE `Article reference` HAS
    number    IS A NUMBER
    letter    IS A STRING
    paragraph IS A NUMBER

§ `Shared constants`

-- A date to pass where a question is not about one AI system, together with `Route neither`:
-- the time axis then ignores it. It is not a legal date.
`the date to pass when no AI system is in question` MEANS Date 1 1 1900

-- The route to pass where the question is not about one AI system.
`the route to pass when no AI system is in question` MEANS `Route neither`
